session "DFS_Skeletons" in DFS = "Directed_Set_Graphs" +
  description \<open>Generic depth-first search skeletons.

    DFS_Skeleton is the skeleton itself: a stack-based search over a Pair_Graph_Specs adjacency
    map, parameterized by callbacks -- among them on_push, applied to the freshly-pushed state in
    the descend step, so that an instance can maintain data that changes when a vertex enters the
    stack -- with its termination argument and the invariants (seen/finished, the stack shape)
    proved once and for all. Two sibling refinements move the adjacency map into the state so a
    callback may prune it. DFS_Skeleton_Refine prunes at push, so the neighbourhoods hold the
    unseen out-neighbours and the loop is proved equivalent to the one that reads a fixed map.
    DFS_Skeleton_Refine_Unfin prunes at backtrack, so the neighbourhoods hold the unfinished
    out-neighbours -- what a back-edge-hunting instance needs; since the offered set may then
    contain on-stack vertices, the instance's found must police the selection (the sel_unseen
    obligation), and the reasoning layer proves the structural invariants and termination
    directly against the refined loop rather than by equivalence to the fixed-map one.

    Kept as a light session of its own so that consumers needing only a DFS skeleton -- e.g. the
    directed-cycle detection in Directed_Cycle_DFS, or an external development such as a datalog
    foundedness check -- need not depend on Graph_Algorithms_Dev, which pulls in
    Verified_SAT_Based_AI_Planning, BFS and Bellman-Ford.\<close>
  sessions
    AI_Provenance
  theories
    DFS_Skeleton
    DFS_Skeleton_Refine
    DFS_Skeleton_Refine_Unfin

session "Directed_Cycle_DFS" in Directed_Cycles = "DFS_Skeletons" +
  description \<open>Directed-cycle detection by depth-first search.

    DFS_DirCycle is the single-root directed-cycle detector on the DFS_Skeletons skeleton: a cycle
    exists below the root iff the search meets a back edge, i.e. an edge into a seen but unfinished
    vertex. Graph_Topological_Order gives the certificate view -- a finite directed graph has a
    topological numbering iff it has no directed cycle, which is the usual way to certify
    acyclicity, checkable in one pass over the edges.

    On top of that sits a whole-graph (linear) directed-cycle search, the directed counterpart of
    DFS_Cycles: an outer sweep over the vertices calling the detector once per remaining root, each
    call pre-seeded with the region earlier calls already finished, so the whole run is linear
    rather than one fresh DFS per vertex. It is developed as a chain of refinements, each level
    removing one set operation from a hot loop and each justified by an equivalence back to the
    level below it, so that the correctness argument is stated once against the simplest version and
    inherited by the fastest one. Every level ships an inner pre-seeded DFS (..._Aux) and an outer
    sweep that calls it once per remaining root.

      * Level 0 -- DFS_DirCycle_Linear{_Aux}, on DFS_Skeleton and DFS_DirCycle unchanged, i.e. with
        the identity on_push. The reference implementation: the back-edge test recomputes
        "seen - finished" at every skeleton step, and the outer loop recomputes "V - finished" at
        every iteration.
      * Level 1 -- DFS_DirCycle_Linear_Tracked{_Aux}, the first level to use on_push. The gray
        (on-stack) and unfinished sets become state components maintained incrementally, so both of
        level 0's differences disappear. DFS_DirCycle_Linear_Tracked_Aux_Equiv relates the two inner searches,
        step for step; DFS_DirCycle_Linear_Tracked_Equiv relates the two outer sweeps, which agree
        on the verdict but need not visit in the same order.\<close>
  sessions
    AI_Provenance
  theories
    DFS_DirCycle
    Graph_Topological_Order
    DFS_DirCycle_Linear_Aux
    DFS_DirCycle_Linear
    DFS_DirCycle_Linear_Tracked_Aux
    DFS_DirCycle_Linear_Tracked_Aux_Equiv
    DFS_DirCycle_Linear_Tracked
    DFS_DirCycle_Linear_Tracked_Equiv

